1
00:00:00,102 --> 00:00:05,241
In 1848, a twelve-year-old boy stepped off
a ship in America with nothing.

2
00:00:06,042 --> 00:00:09,579
Within a few years he was earning one
dollar and twenty cents a week,

3
00:00:09,753 --> 00:00:12,314
breathing cotton dust in a factory
basement.

4
00:00:13,112 --> 00:00:18,551
Fifty-three years later, that same boy
sold his company for four hundred and
eighty million dollars,

5
00:00:18,737 --> 00:00:23,433
and the banker buying it said six words:
"You are now the richest man in the
world."

6
00:00:24,232 --> 00:00:26,103
His name was Andrew Carnegie.

7
00:00:26,901 --> 00:00:31,689
His steel built the bridges, the railroads
and the skyscrapers of a new country.

8
00:00:32,487 --> 00:00:36,532
And then, before he died, he gave away
almost every dollar.

9
00:00:37,331 --> 00:00:38,681
Was it generosity?

10
00:00:39,479 --> 00:00:43,342
Or was it the most brilliant cover-up in
the history of American business?

11
00:00:44,141 --> 00:00:48,798
To understand the man who gave it all
away, you first have to see how he took it
all.

12
00:00:49,596 --> 00:00:51,780
This is the story of Andrew Carnegie.

13
00:00:52,686 --> 00:00:59,192
Andrew Carnegie was born on November
twenty-fifth, eighteen thirty-five, in
Dunfermline, Scotland.

14
00:00:59,993 --> 00:01:06,968
His father, William, was a handloom
weaver, a proud craftsman who worked at a
wooden loom in the family's one-room home.

15
00:01:07,766 --> 00:01:10,210
Then the steam-powered factories arrived.

16
00:01:11,009 --> 00:01:14,559
A machine could do in an hour what William
needed a day to finish,

17
00:01:14,693 --> 00:01:16,317
and the orders simply stopped.

18
00:01:17,115 --> 00:01:20,379
The family went from comfortable to
desperate in a few years.

19
00:01:21,178 --> 00:01:23,465
Young Andrew watched his father break.

20
00:01:24,264 --> 00:01:25,523
He never forgot it.

21
00:01:26,321 --> 00:01:29,520
Later he wrote that he wanted to be the
one who owned the machines,

22
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:31,382
not the one crushed by them.

23
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:38,609
In eighteen forty-eight, the family sold
what little they had, borrowed money, and
sailed to Pennsylvania.

24
00:01:39,407 --> 00:01:40,809
Andrew was twelve.

25
00:01:41,607 --> 00:01:44,546
His first job was in a cotton mill near
Pittsburgh.

26
00:01:45,344 --> 00:01:51,421
He changed bobbins for twelve hours a day,
six days a week, for a dollar and twenty
cents.

27
00:01:52,219 --> 00:01:54,846
But the boy had something the other
workers didn't.

28
00:01:55,644 --> 00:01:57,267
He wasn't planning to stay.

29
00:01:58,182 --> 00:02:03,790
At thirteen, Carnegie got a better job:
messenger boy at a Pittsburgh telegraph
office,

30
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:06,212
for two dollars and fifty cents a week.

31
00:02:07,012 --> 00:02:12,711
While other boys wasted their free time,
he memorized every street and every
business in the city.

32
00:02:13,510 --> 00:02:19,026
He learned the names of the powerful men
who received his messages, and he made
sure they remembered his.

33
00:02:19,825 --> 00:02:24,925
Then he taught himself to read the
telegraph by sound, something almost
nobody could do.

34
00:02:25,723 --> 00:02:27,425
Soon he was an operator.

35
00:02:28,223 --> 00:02:33,375
One of the men who noticed was Thomas
Scott, a top executive at the Pennsylvania
Railroad,

36
00:02:33,575 --> 00:02:35,394
the biggest company in America.

37
00:02:36,192 --> 00:02:41,748
In eighteen fifty-three, Scott hired the
eighteen-year-old Carnegie as his personal
secretary.

38
00:02:42,546 --> 00:02:45,120
It was the best education money couldn't
buy.

39
00:02:45,932 --> 00:02:53,961
Carnegie sat beside the man who ran a
giant, and learned how costs, schedules
and people were managed on a scale nobody
had ever seen.

40
00:02:54,760 --> 00:03:00,420
And Scott would soon give him something
worth far more than a salary: his first
chance to become rich.

41
00:03:01,326 --> 00:03:04,330
Scott let his young protégé in on
investments.

42
00:03:05,130 --> 00:03:08,941
The first was shares in Adams Express, a
delivery company.

43
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:14,423
Carnegie didn't have the money, so his
mother mortgaged the family home to help
him buy in.

44
00:03:15,221 --> 00:03:16,350
It paid off.

45
00:03:17,149 --> 00:03:22,301
The dividends arrived, and the boy who
changed bobbins now had an income from
capital.

46
00:03:23,099 --> 00:03:24,749
Then came the Civil War.

47
00:03:25,547 --> 00:03:33,368
Scott became a senior official in
Washington, and Carnegie went with him to
run the military telegraph and the
railroads that moved the Union army.

48
00:03:34,167 --> 00:03:38,056
He learned how a war machine runs on iron,
rails and speed.

49
00:03:38,854 --> 00:03:42,314
The war made iron the most valuable
material in America.

50
00:03:43,112 --> 00:03:52,301
Carnegie put his money into iron works, a
sleeping-car company, a bridge-building
firm, and a small Pennsylvania oil farm
that paid enormous dividends.

51
00:03:53,099 --> 00:03:59,110
By the time he left the railroad in
eighteen sixty-five, he was already a
wealthy man in his twenties.

52
00:03:59,909 --> 00:04:01,832
But iron was only the beginning.

53
00:04:02,630 --> 00:04:06,715
Across the ocean, he was about to see
something that would change everything.

54
00:04:07,638 --> 00:04:13,532
In eighteen seventy-two, Carnegie visited
England and watched a new process called
the Bessemer converter.

55
00:04:14,333 --> 00:04:19,068
A blast of air turned molten iron into
steel in minutes, instead of days.

56
00:04:19,867 --> 00:04:23,053
Steel was stronger than iron and far more
durable.

57
00:04:23,851 --> 00:04:25,748
Everyone knew it would be important.

58
00:04:26,546 --> 00:04:28,717
Carnegie understood it would be
everything.

59
00:04:29,515 --> 00:04:33,964
Back in Pittsburgh, he did what most
businessmen of the time were afraid to do:

60
00:04:34,190 --> 00:04:35,123
he bet it all.

61
00:04:35,921 --> 00:04:41,204
He sold his other interests and poured the
money into a giant new mill on the
Monongahela River.

62
00:04:42,002 --> 00:04:46,594
He named it the Edgar Thomson Works, after
the president of the Pennsylvania
Railroad,

63
00:04:46,768 --> 00:04:48,404
his biggest future customer.

64
00:04:49,203 --> 00:04:51,568
It was a very deliberate piece of
flattery.

65
00:04:52,367 --> 00:04:57,415
The mill opened in eighteen seventy-five,
just as the country was sinking into a
depression.

66
00:04:58,213 --> 00:04:59,628
Carnegie didn't care.

67
00:05:00,427 --> 00:05:03,482
When competitors cut production, he kept
building.

68
00:05:04,281 --> 00:05:10,462
His rule was simple: cut costs, cut
prices, and take the customers of everyone
who couldn't keep up.

69
00:05:11,382 --> 00:05:13,422
Carnegie's genius was control.

70
00:05:14,223 --> 00:05:15,794
He didn't just make steel.

71
00:05:16,593 --> 00:05:22,904
He bought the iron ore fields, the coal
and coke supply, the ships and the
railroads that carried them.

72
00:05:23,702 --> 00:05:27,200
Every step between the ground and the
finished beam belonged to him.

73
00:05:27,999 --> 00:05:30,859
No one else in America was doing it at
this scale.

74
00:05:31,658 --> 00:05:35,755
When a supplier raised prices, Carnegie
simply owned the supplier.

75
00:05:36,554 --> 00:05:38,307
He obsessed over numbers.

76
00:05:39,106 --> 00:05:42,786
Every night, he wanted to know the exact
cost of every ton.

77
00:05:43,585 --> 00:05:49,818
Managers who couldn't cut costs were
replaced, and those who could became
partners, bound to him by shares.

78
00:05:50,616 --> 00:05:56,042
In eighteen eighty-nine he brought in
Henry Clay Frick, the coke king, and made
him chairman.

79
00:05:56,840 --> 00:06:00,859
Frick was a hard man who believed workers
were an expense to be managed.

80
00:06:01,658 --> 00:06:06,471
By nineteen hundred, Carnegie's mills were
making more steel than all of Great
Britain.

81
00:06:07,270 --> 00:06:09,114
But efficiency has a price.

82
00:06:09,913 --> 00:06:16,458
Workers in the mills labored twelve-hour
shifts, seven days a week, and the pay was
falling as profits climbed.

83
00:06:17,257 --> 00:06:18,815
Something was going to break.

84
00:06:19,734 --> 00:06:25,720
In eighteen ninety-two, the contract with
the union at Carnegie's Homestead plant
was about to expire.

85
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:31,737
Carnegie, who liked to call himself a
friend of working men, left for his castle
in Scotland.

86
00:06:32,536 --> 00:06:34,185
He left Frick in charge.

87
00:06:34,984 --> 00:06:36,842
Frick wanted to break the union.

88
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:43,521
He cut wages, built a fence topped with
barbed wire around the plant, and locked
the workers out.

89
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:49,081
Then he hired three hundred armed
Pinkerton agents and sent them up the
river on barges,

90
00:06:49,215 --> 00:06:51,685
at night, to take the mill by force.

91
00:06:52,484 --> 00:06:53,847
The workers were waiting.

92
00:06:54,645 --> 00:06:57,974
On July sixth, a battle broke out on the
riverbank.

93
00:06:58,773 --> 00:07:01,894
Guns, dynamite, burning oil on the water.

94
00:07:02,692 --> 00:07:05,552
By the end, men on both sides were dead.

95
00:07:06,351 --> 00:07:12,258
The state militia arrived, the strike was
crushed, and the union was effectively
broken at Homestead.

96
00:07:13,057 --> 00:07:15,409
Carnegie was thousands of miles away.

97
00:07:16,208 --> 00:07:18,977
He had always claimed to support workers'
rights.

98
00:07:19,775 --> 00:07:24,146
Yet he never repudiated what was done in
his name, and the steel kept flowing.

99
00:07:24,945 --> 00:07:26,516
The public never forgot.

100
00:07:27,314 --> 00:07:30,891
The man who preached about uplifting the
poor had just crushed them.

101
00:07:31,806 --> 00:07:36,125
By the end of the century, Carnegie was in
his sixties and tired of the fight.

102
00:07:36,925 --> 00:07:37,963
He wanted out.

103
00:07:38,761 --> 00:07:45,098
At a dinner in eighteen ninety-nine, a
young steel man casually asked what
Carnegie's company might be worth.

104
00:07:45,897 --> 00:07:50,645
The idea spread, and it reached the most
powerful banker in America: J.P.

105
00:07:50,558 --> 00:07:51,205
Morgan.

106
00:07:52,004 --> 00:07:55,658
Morgan wanted to merge the steel industry
into a single giant.

107
00:07:56,457 --> 00:07:59,669
Carnegie's company was the one piece he
couldn't build without.

108
00:08:00,467 --> 00:08:05,281
Carnegie scribbled a number on a piece of
paper: four hundred and eighty million
dollars.

109
00:08:06,079 --> 00:08:08,861
Morgan glanced at it and said, "I accept."

110
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:16,049
In nineteen oh-one, the deal created
United States Steel, the first billion-
dollar corporation in history.

111
00:08:16,847 --> 00:08:20,749
Carnegie's share was roughly two hundred
and twenty-five million dollars.

112
00:08:21,548 --> 00:08:26,570
As the story goes, Morgan shook his hand
and said, "Congratulations, Mr.

113
00:08:26,483 --> 00:08:27,195
Carnegie.

114
00:08:27,993 --> 00:08:30,294
You are now the richest man in the world."

115
00:08:31,092 --> 00:08:33,106
Carnegie had reached the very top.

116
00:08:33,905 --> 00:08:38,341
And then, at sixty-six years old, he did
something no one expected.

117
00:08:39,246 --> 00:08:44,711
Years earlier, in eighteen eighty-nine,
Carnegie had written an essay that shocked
the rich.

118
00:08:45,511 --> 00:08:47,708
He called it "The Gospel of Wealth."

119
00:08:48,506 --> 00:08:53,255
Its central idea was brutal: "The man who
dies thus rich dies disgraced."

120
00:08:54,053 --> 00:08:56,393
He argued that a fortune was a trust.

121
00:08:57,191 --> 00:09:02,369
The wealthy had a duty to spend it, during
their lifetime, on things that lifted
society.

122
00:09:03,168 --> 00:09:04,335
Now he meant it.

123
00:09:05,134 --> 00:09:07,551
He retired and went to work giving it
away.

124
00:09:08,350 --> 00:09:15,598
He funded more than two thousand five
hundred public libraries across the world,
a concert hall in New York that still
carries his name,

125
00:09:15,746 --> 00:09:20,728
universities, research institutes, and a
palace in The Hague dedicated to peace.

126
00:09:21,527 --> 00:09:27,564
By the time he died, in nineteen nineteen,
he had given away roughly ninety percent
of his fortune.

127
00:09:28,363 --> 00:09:30,624
But the twist is what the money couldn't
buy.

128
00:09:31,436 --> 00:09:37,200
Many of those libraries stood in mill
towns where the men who filled his
furnaces worked twelve-hour days.

129
00:09:37,998 --> 00:09:39,270
He gave them books.

130
00:09:40,069 --> 00:09:43,736
Critics said he should have given them
higher wages and shorter hours.

131
00:09:44,535 --> 00:09:46,627
The question follows him to this day.

132
00:09:47,425 --> 00:09:51,054
Was the giving an act of conscience, or
the price of a clean name?

133
00:09:51,966 --> 00:09:54,332
Andrew Carnegie was two men at once.

134
00:09:55,132 --> 00:09:59,633
He was the boy who understood that whoever
controls the process controls the profit,

135
00:09:59,833 --> 00:10:03,722
and he spent forty years building a
machine that no competitor could match.

136
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:10,245
And he was the man who, once he had won,
understood that a fortune means nothing if
it dies with you.

137
00:10:11,044 --> 00:10:13,383
His story holds a warning and a gift.

138
00:10:14,182 --> 00:10:20,792
The warning: efficiency without conscience
crushes people, and a library can't undo a
battle on a riverbank.

139
00:10:21,591 --> 00:10:24,672
The gift: wealth is only worth what you
build with it.

140
00:10:25,471 --> 00:10:28,383
The poor weaver's son changed a country
twice.

141
00:10:29,182 --> 00:10:32,055
Once with steel, and once with generosity.

142
00:10:32,854 --> 00:10:34,594
Which one will history remember?

143
00:10:35,393 --> 00:10:37,016
That, I'll leave to you.

144
00:10:37,815 --> 00:10:40,297
If you enjoyed this story, subscribe.

145
00:10:41,096 --> 00:10:45,688
Next time, we look at another empire built
on ambition, and what it cost.

